# Full studies: Scripture and Tradition; Peter and the Church. Loaded by tools/vapol.py (exec).

D('2-timothy',3,16,
 ctx="2 Timothy is Paul’s last letter, written from prison in Rome to his disciple Timothy, whom he had placed over the church at Ephesus. Chapter 3 warns of ‘dangerous times’ and false teachers (3:1–9). Then: ‘But continue thou in those things which thou hast learned, and which have been committed to thee: knowing of whom thou hast learned them; and because from thy infancy thou hast known the holy scriptures, which can instruct thee to salvation, by the faith which is in Christ Jesus. All scripture, inspired of God, is profitable to teach, to reprove, to correct, to instruct in justice, that the man of God may be perfect, furnished to every good work’ (3:14–17). Chapter 4 then commands Timothy to ‘preach the word’ (4:2). Earlier Paul had told him: ‘the things which thou hast heard of me by many witnesses, the same commend to faithful men, who shall be fit to teach others also’ (2:2).",
 who="This is the central proof-text for sola scriptura, the Reformation principle that Scripture is the only infallible rule of faith: all that is necessary for salvation is contained in Scripture, either explicitly or by necessary inference (Westminster Confession, 1646, I.6). The argument: Scripture is God-breathed; it makes the man of God ‘complete, thoroughly equipped for every good work’; if Scripture equips completely, nothing else (Tradition, Magisterium) is needed as a rule of faith.\n\nThe strongest form does not deny that tradition and church authority exist; it says they are fallible and subordinate, while only Scripture is ‘God-breathed’.",
 lang="“Inspired of God” (theopneustos): literally ‘God-breathed’. Catholics fully affirm it (Dei Verbum 11).\n\n“Profitable” (ōphelimos): useful, beneficial. It does not mean ‘sufficient’ (hikanos).\n\n“Perfect” (artios) and “furnished” (exērtismenos): fit, complete, equipped. The same kind of language is used elsewhere of other means: ‘let patience have its perfect work; that you may be perfect (teleioi) and entire (holoklēroi), failing in nothing’ (James 1:4).\n\n“The man of God” is a title for a minister of God (1 Timothy 6:11; used of prophets in the Old Testament): Paul is speaking of Timothy’s equipment as a pastor.",
 steps=[
  ["Catholics affirm that all Scripture is God-breathed","The Church teaches that the books of Scripture have God as their author and teach ‘solidly, faithfully and without error that truth which God wanted put into sacred writings for the sake of salvation’ (Dei Verbum 11). The disagreement is not about inspiration but about whether Scripture is the only rule of faith."],
  ["‘Profitable’ is not ‘sufficient’","Paul says Scripture is useful for teaching, reproof, correction and training. Something can be necessary and useful without being the only thing needed. Water is profitable for life, and you still need food. The verse never says ‘only Scripture’."],
  ["The verse proves too much, if it proves sola scriptura","The Scriptures Timothy knew ‘from infancy’ (3:15) were the Old Testament. Much of the New Testament had not yet been written, and no list of New Testament books existed. If 3:16 means ‘Scripture alone is sufficient’, then the Old Testament alone was sufficient, and the New Testament unnecessary. No Protestant accepts that conclusion."],
  ["‘Perfect, furnished’ does not exclude other means","James 1:4 says patience makes us ‘perfect and entire, failing in nothing’. No one concludes that patience alone suffices for the Christian life. Ephesians 4:11–13 says Christ gave apostles, prophets, evangelists, pastors and teachers ‘for the perfecting of the saints… unto a perfect man’. If ‘equipped’ language makes Scripture sole, it makes pastors sole too."],
  ["The same letter commands oral tradition","‘The things which thou hast heard of me by many witnesses, the same commend to faithful men, who shall be fit to teach others also’ (2:2): four generations of oral transmission (Paul, Timothy, faithful men, others). 3:14: ‘continue in those things which thou hast learned… knowing of whom thou hast learned them’. And 2 Thessalonians 2:14: ‘hold the traditions, which you have learned, whether by word, or by our epistle’."],
  ["Scripture does not tell us which books are Scripture","No inspired list of the 27 New Testament books exists. Some books were disputed for centuries (Hebrews, James, 2 Peter, 2 and 3 John, Jude, Revelation). The canon was recognized by the Church: the earliest list matching our 27 books is Athanasius’ Festal Letter 39 (367); councils at Rome (382), Hippo (393) and Carthage (397) set out the canon. Sola scriptura depends on a list that Scripture itself does not provide."],
  ["Scripture itself points to a living authority","‘The church of the living God, the pillar and ground of the truth’ (1 Timothy 3:15). ‘If he will not hear the church, let him be to thee as the heathen’ (Matthew 18:17). In Acts 15 a disputed question is settled by the apostles and elders in council: ‘it hath seemed good to the Holy Ghost and to us’ (15:28). Scripture never says the Church’s teaching authority will cease when the last apostle dies."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Irenaeus, Against Heresies III.4.1 (c. 180)","Even if the apostles had left no writings, we should follow the order of tradition they handed to those to whom they entrusted the churches; many barbarian peoples believe in Christ without paper or ink, keeping the ancient tradition (paraphrased)."],
  ["Basil the Great, On the Holy Spirit 27.66 (c. 375)","Of the beliefs and practices preserved in the Church, some come from written teaching, others from apostolic tradition handed down ‘in a mystery’; both have the same force (paraphrased). Examples he gives: the sign of the Cross, facing east in prayer, the words of the epiclesis."],
  ["Vincent of Lérins, Commonitorium 2.5 (434)","The canon of Scripture is complete and sufficient, yet because people interpret it in different ways, it must be read according to the Church’s tradition: what has been believed everywhere, always, by all (paraphrased)."],
  ["Athanasius, Festal Letter 39 (367)","The first surviving list of exactly the 27 books of the New Testament."],
  ["Second Vatican Council, Dei Verbum 9–10 (1965)","Scripture and Tradition flow from the same divine wellspring; the Magisterium is not above the Word of God but serves it."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Tradition can be corrupted; Scripture can’t.”","Scripture cannot be corrupted, but interpretations of it can, and have been, which is why there are thousands of Protestant denominations disagreeing about baptism, the Eucharist, predestination and church order, all claiming Scripture alone. The question is not whether Scripture is infallible but who has authority to interpret it when Christians disagree (2 Peter 3:16: ‘things hard to be understood, which the unlearned and unstable wrest… to their own destruction’)."],
  ["“Paul’s oral traditions were later written down in the New Testament.”","That is an assumption, not something Scripture says. Paul never says that his oral teaching would all be written, or that the written record would replace the living teaching office he set up in each city (Titus 1:5). And the Church’s acceptance of infant baptism, Sunday worship, the canon itself and the Trinitarian formulation of the creeds rely on tradition interpreting Scripture."],
  ["“The Bereans tested Paul by Scripture (Acts 17:11).”","See the note on Acts 17:11: they checked whether the Old Testament supported an oral apostolic message, then accepted that message, which was more than the Old Testament contained."],
  ["“Jesus rebuked tradition (Mark 7).”","He rebuked human traditions that contradicted God’s law. See the note on Mark 7:8."]],
 talk="“I believe every word of 2 Timothy 3:16: all Scripture is God-breathed. But it says Scripture is ‘profitable’, not that it’s the only authority. And the Scripture Timothy knew from childhood was the Old Testament; if this verse meant ‘Scripture alone is enough’, he’d have no need of the New Testament. In the same letter Paul tells Timothy to pass on what he heard from Paul orally to faithful men who will teach others (2:2), and in 2 Thessalonians he says to hold the traditions ‘whether by word or by letter’. So the Bible itself points to Scripture and apostolic Tradition together, with the Church as ‘the pillar and foundation of the truth’.”",
 avoid="Do not say Catholics believe Tradition is a second set of doctrines unrelated to Scripture; Dei Verbum calls them one deposit. Do not suggest the Church ‘created’ the Bible; she recognized and received the inspired books. And do not overclaim the Council of Rome (382): its list survives in a later document whose dating is debated.",
 read=["Second Vatican Council, Dei Verbum 7–10 (1965)","CCC 74–100, 120","Council of Trent, Session 4 (1546)","Irenaeus, Against Heresies III.1–4","Basil, On the Holy Spirit 27","Yves Congar (1966) Tradition and Traditions. London: Burns & Oates","Keith Mathison (2001) The Shape of Sola Scriptura. Moscow, ID: Canon Press (a careful Protestant defence, for comparison)"])

D('mark',7,8,
 ctx="Mark 7:1–13. Pharisees and scribes from Jerusalem see Jesus’ disciples eating with unwashed hands, against ‘the tradition of the ancients’ (7:3). Mark explains for Gentile readers the Pharisaic washings of hands, cups and pots (7:3–4). Jesus answers with Isaiah 29:13 (‘This people honoureth me with their lips…’) and then: ‘For leaving the commandment of God, you hold the tradition of men, the washing of pots and of cups… Well do you make void the commandment of God, that you may keep your own tradition’ (7:8–9). His example: the custom of ‘Corban’, by which a son could declare his goods dedicated to God and so avoid supporting his parents, breaking the fourth commandment (7:10–13).",
 who="Jesus condemns ‘the tradition of men’. Catholic tradition (Mary’s assumption, purgatory, indulgences, the papacy, devotions) is said to be exactly this: human additions that make void God’s word. True Christians follow Scripture alone and reject human traditions.",
 lang="“Tradition” (paradosis) means ‘that which is handed over’. The New Testament uses it negatively for human customs that contradict God (Mark 7:8–9, 13; Colossians 2:8) and positively for apostolic teaching: ‘keep my ordinances (paradoseis) as I have delivered them to you’ (1 Corinthians 11:2); ‘hold the traditions (paradoseis)’ (2 Thessalonians 2:14); ‘withdraw yourselves from every brother walking disorderly, and not according to the tradition (paradosin) which they have received of us’ (2 Thessalonians 3:6).\n\n“Of men” (tōn anthrōpōn) is the key qualifier.",
 steps=[
  ["Jesus condemns traditions that nullify God’s commandment","His charge is precise: ‘you make void the commandment of God, that you may keep your own tradition’ (7:9). The Corban custom cancelled the duty to honour parents. Any tradition that contradicts God’s word stands condemned. Catholics agree."],
  ["The New Testament praises apostolic tradition","The same word, paradosis, is used with approval for what the apostles handed on (1 Corinthians 11:2; 2 Thessalonians 2:14; 3:6). Paul ‘delivered’ (paredōka) the Eucharist (1 Corinthians 11:23) and the creed of the resurrection (15:3). The distinction is between traditions of men and the tradition of God given through Christ and the apostles."],
  ["Jesus himself honoured Jewish tradition where it was faithful","‘The scribes and the Pharisees have sitten on the chair of Moses. All things therefore whatsoever they shall say to you, observe and do’ (Matthew 23:2–3). The ‘chair of Moses’ is not in the Old Testament; it is a tradition of teaching authority. Jesus attended synagogue on the Sabbath (Luke 4:16), a practice developed after the Exile, and the feast of Dedication (John 10:22), which comes from the Maccabees, not the Torah."],
  ["The New Testament draws on oral tradition","Jude 9 mentions Michael disputing with the devil over Moses’ body; 2 Timothy 3:8 names Jannes and Jambres, Pharaoh’s magicians; 1 Corinthians 10:4 speaks of the rock that ‘followed’ Israel; Matthew 2:23 cites a prophecy (‘he shall be called a Nazarene’) found in no prophetic book. None of these is in the Old Testament."],
  ["Test each Catholic doctrine, don’t assume","The fair question is whether a given Catholic teaching contradicts Scripture as Corban did. Purgatory, praying for the dead, honouring Mary, the papacy: Catholics argue each is rooted in Scripture and early tradition and contradicts none of it (see the other notes). Labelling them ‘traditions of men’ assumes what needs to be proved."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Irenaeus, Against Heresies III.3.1–3 (c. 180)","The tradition of the apostles is manifest in every church, through the succession of bishops; he lists the bishops of Rome from Peter and Paul to Eleutherius (paraphrased)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, Catena Aurea on Mark 7","Collects patristic comments that the Lord condemns traditions which, invented by men, are preferred to God’s commandments (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“All extra-biblical tradition is ‘of men’.”","Then the Christian canon, the doctrine of the Trinity in its developed form, Sunday worship and infant baptism would also be ‘traditions of men’, since Scripture does not explicitly define them. Most Protestants accept these because the early Church handed them on."],
  ["“Paul’s traditions were later fully written down.”","Possibly some were; but Scripture never says all were, and 2 Thessalonians 2:14 names both channels side by side."]],
 talk="“Jesus condemned traditions that cancel God’s commandments, like Corban, which let people dodge supporting their parents. Catholics agree; any custom like that is wrong. But the same word ‘tradition’ is used positively by Paul: ‘hold the traditions you were taught, whether by word or by letter’ (2 Thessalonians 2:15 in most Bibles). So the question isn’t whether tradition exists, but whose: human customs against God’s word, or what the apostles handed on.”",
 avoid="Do not deny that some popular Catholic customs have become superstitious at times; the Church herself has reformed them (Trent, Session 25). Distinguish dogma from piety.",
 read=["Dei Verbum 7–10","CCC 75–83","Yves Congar (1966) Tradition and Traditions","Irenaeus, Against Heresies III.1–4"])

D('matthew',15,9,
 ctx="Matthew 15:1–20 is Matthew’s version of the dispute in Mark 7. Pharisees ask why the disciples ‘transgress the tradition of the ancients’ by not washing their hands. Jesus replies: ‘Why do you also transgress the commandment of God for your tradition?’ (15:3), gives the Corban example (15:4–6), and quotes Isaiah 29:13: ‘This people honoureth me with their lips: but their heart is far from me. And in vain do they worship me, teaching doctrines and commandments of men’ (15:8–9). He then teaches that what defiles a man comes from the heart (15:10–20).",
 who="Used like Mark 7:8: Catholic doctrines are ‘commandments of men’, so Catholic worship is ‘in vain’. Often applied to rules such as Friday abstinence, clerical celibacy, holy days of obligation and the Rosary.",
 lang="Jesus quotes the Greek Old Testament of Isaiah 29:13: didaskontes didaskalias entalmata anthrōpōn, ‘teaching as doctrines the precepts of men’. The fault is presenting human precepts as if they were God’s, while the heart is far from God.",
 steps=[
  ["The target is hypocrisy and the cancelling of God’s law","Isaiah’s charge is lip-service with a distant heart. Jesus applies it to leaders who use their customs to escape God’s commandment. Catholics accept the warning for themselves: external religion without a converted heart is vain (CCC 2111; Isaiah 1:11–17)."],
  ["Jesus gave his apostles authority to bind and loose","‘Whatsoever you shall bind upon earth, shall be bound also in heaven’ (Matthew 18:18; 16:19). In rabbinic language ‘binding and loosing’ meant declaring what is forbidden or permitted. Christ gave this authority to the apostles, and they used it: the Council of Jerusalem bound Gentile Christians to abstain from blood and things strangled (Acts 15:28–29), a rule not found in any Gospel."],
  ["Church disciplines are not passed off as divine doctrine","The Church distinguishes clearly between divine law (unchangeable) and ecclesiastical law (changeable disciplines such as Friday penance, fasting rules, celibacy in the Latin Church). She does not claim that Friday abstinence is a commandment from Sinai; she binds it, as the apostles did in Acts 15, for the good of souls (CCC 2041–2043). That is the opposite of what Isaiah condemns."],
  ["Every Christian community has ‘precepts’","Protestant churches set the time of Sunday worship, communion practice, membership rules and pastors’ qualifications. These are not in Scripture in detail either. Authority to order the community for worship is biblical (1 Corinthians 14:40; Hebrews 13:17)."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Jerome, Commentary on Matthew, on 15:9 (398)","The Lord condemns those who prefer the traditions of the Pharisees to the commandments of God, as in the matter of honouring parents (paraphrased)."],
  ["Augustine, Letter 54 to Januarius 1.1 (c. 400)","Some customs are observed throughout the world by apostolic tradition or by councils; others vary by place. Where a custom is neither against faith nor morals, follow the custom of the church where you are (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Celibacy and fasting rules are exactly commandments of men.”","They are commandments of the Church, which has authority from Christ to bind (Matthew 18:18), and they contradict no commandment of God. Paul himself praises celibacy (1 Corinthians 7:7–8, 32–35), and Jesus said his disciples would fast (Matthew 9:15)."]],
 talk="“Jesus is quoting Isaiah against people who honoured God with their lips while using their customs to avoid God’s actual commandments. That warning is for all of us. But Jesus also gave his apostles authority to bind and loose, and they used it: in Acts 15 they made rules for Gentile Christians that aren’t in the Gospels. When the Church sets a fasting rule, she isn’t claiming it came from Sinai; she’s using that same authority for our good.”",
 avoid="Do not present disciplines (like Friday abstinence) as dogmas. That actually would be the error the verse condemns.",
 read=["CCC 1951–1974 (law), 2041–2043 (precepts of the Church)","Acts 15","Augustine, Letter 54"])

D('revelation',22,18,
 ctx="These are the last words of the last book of the Bible. ‘For I testify to every one that heareth the words of the prophecy of this book: If any man shall add to these things, God shall add unto him the plagues written in this book. And if any man shall take away from the words of the book of this prophecy, God shall take away his part out of the book of life, and out of the holy city’ (22:18–19).",
 who="This warning is applied to the whole Bible: nothing may be added to God’s word. Catholic dogmas not found in Scripture (the Assumption, the Immaculate Conception, papal infallibility, purgatory) are said to ‘add to’ Scripture and fall under this curse.",
 lang="“This book” (tou bibliou toutou) and ‘the words of the prophecy of this book’ refer to the book of Revelation itself, the scroll John was told to write (1:11, 19). When Revelation was written, the books of the New Testament had not been gathered into one volume; there was no ‘Bible’ in our sense for ‘this book’ to mean.",
 steps=[
  ["The warning protects the text of Revelation","Ancient authors and copyists sometimes added warnings to protect a document from tampering. John warns copyists and readers not to alter this prophecy. The Church agrees entirely and has never added words to Revelation."],
  ["Moses said the same thing, and many books followed","‘You shall not add to the word that I speak to you, neither shall you take away from it’ (Deuteronomy 4:2; 12:32). If that meant ‘no more inspired writing’, the Prophets, Psalms and the whole New Testament would be forbidden additions. It meant: do not alter what God has given."],
  ["Proverbs 30:6 is the same principle","‘Add not any thing to his words, lest thou be reproved, and found a liar.’ Yet Proverbs was followed by many inspired books. The principle forbids falsifying God’s word, not receiving more of it."],
  ["Doctrinal definitions do not add to revelation","When the Church defines a dogma, she does not claim new revelation: public revelation ended with the apostles (Dei Verbum 4; CCC 66). She declares what was always part of the deposit of faith, received in Scripture and Tradition, as the Council of Nicaea did when it defined that the Son is ‘consubstantial’ with the Father, a word not in Scripture. Protestants accept Nicaea’s ‘addition’ of homoousios for the same reason."],
  ["‘Taking away’ cuts both ways","The verse also warns against taking away. The Reformers removed seven books that had been read in the Church’s Bible for over a thousand years (Tobit, Judith, Wisdom, Sirach, Baruch, 1–2 Maccabees), and Luther initially wanted to relegate James, Jude, Hebrews and Revelation to an appendix. Catholics should not use this to score points, but it shows that the verse is about the integrity of Revelation’s own text."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Irenaeus, Against Heresies V.30.1 (c. 180)","Warns of those who alter the number of the beast in Revelation, and cites the curse on those who add or subtract from the prophecy (paraphrased): an early example of reading it as protecting Revelation’s text."],
  ["CCC 66–67","‘The Christian economy… will never pass away; and no new public revelation is to be expected’; yet the Church grows in understanding of what was revealed."]],
 obj=[
  ["“God providentially placed this warning at the end of the Bible to close the canon.”","The order of books in our Bibles comes from later Christian tradition (the Church’s), not from the authors. Revelation may not even have been the last New Testament book written. To make the warning about the whole canon you must rely on the Church’s ordering of the books."],
  ["“Catholic dogmas are additions.”","Each must be shown to contradict or exceed the apostolic deposit. A definition that explains what was received (as Nicaea explained the Son’s divinity) is not an addition."]],
 talk="“John is talking about ‘the words of the prophecy of this book’: the book of Revelation. Moses said the same in Deuteronomy, ‘don’t add to or subtract from what I command you’, and God still gave the prophets and the New Testament afterwards. It’s a warning not to tamper with the text, not a statement about the whole Bible, which didn’t exist as one book yet.”",
 avoid="Do not imply the Church can create new revelation; she cannot (CCC 66).",
 read=["CCC 65–67, 88–95","Dei Verbum 4, 8","Deuteronomy 4:2; 12:32; Proverbs 30:6"])

D('matthew',16,18,
 ctx="At Caesarea Philippi Jesus asks: ‘Whom do men say that the Son of man is?’ After various answers, ‘Simon Peter answered and said: Thou art Christ, the Son of the living God. And Jesus answering, said to him: Blessed art thou, Simon Bar-Jona: because flesh and blood hath not revealed it to thee, but my Father who is in heaven. And I say to thee: That thou art Peter; and upon this rock I will build my church, and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it. And I will give to thee the keys of the kingdom of heaven. And whatsoever thou shalt bind upon earth, it shall be bound also in heaven: and whatsoever thou shalt loose on earth, it shall be loosed also in heaven’ (16:13–19). A few verses later Jesus rebukes Peter: ‘Go behind me, Satan’ (16:23).",
 who="Protestant readings take the ‘rock’ to be (1) Peter’s confession of faith, (2) Christ himself (1 Corinthians 3:11; 10:4), or (3) Peter, but only personally as the first preacher, with no successors. The popular grammatical argument: Peter is Petros (masculine, ‘a small stone’), while the rock is petra (feminine, ‘bedrock’), so Jesus distinguishes the small stone Peter from the great rock of faith or of Christ. Even granting Peter is the rock, nothing in the text speaks of a succession of bishops in Rome.",
 lang="Jesus spoke Aramaic. John 1:42 gives Peter’s Aramaic name: ‘thou shalt be called Cephas, which is interpreted Peter.’ Kēpha (Cephas) means ‘rock’, and Paul calls him ‘Cephas’ eight times. In Aramaic the sentence would be ‘you are Kēpha, and on this kēpha I will build’, the same word twice.\n\nIn Greek, petra is the ordinary word for rock; to make it a man’s name it had to become masculine, Petros. In earlier classical poetry petros could mean a stone, but in the Koine Greek of the first century the two words were used largely interchangeably. Several Protestant scholars accept that the rock is Peter: Oscar Cullmann (Peter: Disciple, Apostle, Martyr, 1952) and D. A. Carson (Expositor’s Bible Commentary on Matthew, 1984) among them.\n\n“This rock” (tautē tē petra): ‘this’ points back to the nearest referent, Peter just named.\n\n“Keys” (kleidas): the image comes from Isaiah 22:20–22, where God gives Eliakim ‘the key of the house of David… he shall open, and none shall shut’. Eliakim was the ‘one over the house’, the royal steward, an office with predecessors and successors.",
 steps=[
  ["The wordplay requires Peter to be the rock","Jesus gives Simon a new name, ‘Rock’, in the same breath as ‘on this rock I will build’. In Aramaic it is the same word. A play on words that means ‘you are Rock, but I will build on something else’ would be a strange way to bless him. Jesus is answering Peter’s confession with a matching declaration: ‘You said who I am; now I tell you who you are.’"],
  ["Three gifts to one man","Everything in 16:17–19 is addressed to Peter in the singular (‘to thee’, soi): the blessing, the name, the keys, the power to bind and loose. Elsewhere all the apostles receive binding and loosing (18:18), but the keys are given to Peter alone."],
  ["The keys are an office with succession","Isaiah 22: Shebna is removed as royal steward and Eliakim given ‘the key of the house of David’, wearing the steward’s robe and authority. The steward governed the house in the king’s name, and the office passed to successors. Jesus, the Son of David, appoints a steward for his kingdom. An office of this kind does not end with its first holder."],
  ["Peter acts as the leader in the New Testament","He is always named first in lists of the apostles (Matthew 10:2: ‘the first, Simon who is called Peter’). Jesus prays for him that his faith fail not and tells him to confirm his brethren (Luke 22:31–32). The risen Lord tells him three times, ‘Feed my sheep’ (John 21:15–17). He leads the choice of Matthias (Acts 1:15–26), preaches at Pentecost (2:14), opens the Church to the Gentiles (10), and speaks decisively at the Council of Jerusalem (15:7–11)."],
  ["Christ as foundation, Peter as rock: both are true","Scripture uses building images flexibly. Christ is the one foundation (1 Corinthians 3:11) and the cornerstone (Ephesians 2:20); the apostles are the foundation too (Ephesians 2:20; Revelation 21:14, ‘the twelve foundations… the twelve names of the twelve apostles’). Peter is the rock by sharing in Christ’s own stability, as a vicar shares the authority of the one he represents (CCC 881)."],
  ["The Church needed Peter’s office to continue","Jesus promised that the gates of hell would not prevail against his Church, which lasts until the end of the world (Matthew 28:20). If the rock and keys were needed to found and govern the Church in its first generation, they are needed in every generation. The early Church saw the bishop of Rome as holding Peter’s chair: Clement of Rome intervenes in Corinth (c. 96); Irenaeus lists the Roman succession (c. 180); Cyprian calls Rome ‘the chair of Peter’ (Letter 59.14, c. 252)."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Tertullian, On Modesty 21 (c. 220)","Writing as a Montanist critic of the bishop of Rome, he still says the Lord built the Church on Peter and gave him the keys (paraphrased)."],
  ["Cyprian, On the Unity of the Catholic Church 4 (c. 251)","The Lord builds his Church on one man, Peter; though he gives equal power to all the apostles, he establishes one chair, so that the origin of unity might be shown (paraphrased; the text survives in two versions)."],
  ["Augustine, Retractations I.21 (c. 427)","He had earlier said the rock was Peter, later often that it was Christ whom Peter confessed; he leaves the reader to choose which is more probable (paraphrased). Augustine nonetheless affirmed the Roman see’s primacy (Letter 43.7; Against the Letter of Mani 4.5)."],
  ["Leo the Great, Sermon 4 (c. 445)","Peter’s solid faith, praised by the Lord, endures in his see; Peter still governs the Church through his successors (paraphrased)."],
  ["First Vatican Council, Pastor Aeternus ch. 1–2 (1870)","Defines that Peter received a primacy of jurisdiction from Christ, and that by Christ’s institution Peter has perpetual successors, the Roman Pontiffs."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Petros is a small stone, petra a big rock.”","That distinction belongs to older poetic Greek, not to the Koine of Matthew. In any case, Jesus spoke Aramaic, where both are kēpha (John 1:42). The Greek needed a masculine form for a man’s name. Many Protestant scholars now concede this."],
  ["“The rock is Peter’s confession.”","The confession and the confessor are not rivals: Jesus builds on Peter as the one who, by the Father’s revelation, confesses the true faith. That is exactly the Catholic view of the papacy: the pope is the guardian of the confession of Peter."],
  ["“Jesus called Peter ‘Satan’ five verses later.”","Peter is fallible as a man and can sin; Catholics have never denied it. Infallibility is a protection of the Church’s defined teaching, not personal sinlessness. Jesus also warned Peter of his denial, and still made him shepherd (John 21)."],
  ["“Nothing here says anything about Rome or successors.”","The text establishes an office (keys, steward). The early Church identified its holders: Peter died in Rome (1 Peter 5:13; Clement, 1 Clement 5; Ignatius, Romans 4; Irenaeus III.3.2–3; Eusebius, Church History II.25). The succession of the Roman bishops is attested by Irenaeus c. 180."],
  ["“Paul rebuked Peter (Galatians 2:11).”","For inconsistent conduct, not false doctrine; Paul says Peter ‘walked not uprightly unto the truth of the gospel’. Correction of a superior’s behaviour is compatible with his authority (Thomas, ST II-II q.33 a.4 ad 2)."]],
 talk="“Jesus spoke Aramaic, and John tells us Peter’s Aramaic name was Kepha, which means ‘rock’. So Jesus literally said, ‘You are Rock, and on this rock I will build my church.’ Then he gave Peter the keys of the kingdom, which in Isaiah 22 is the symbol of the king’s chief steward, an office that passes to successors. Even well-known Protestant scholars like Oscar Cullmann and D. A. Carson agree the rock is Peter. Catholics just take the next step: an office the Church needs doesn’t disappear when the first holder dies.”",
 avoid="Do not claim every Church Father identified the rock with Peter; Augustine, Chrysostom and others sometimes interpreted it as his faith or as Christ. Do not confuse infallibility with impeccability. And do not argue that every papal act or opinion is infallible; Vatican I defines narrow conditions.",
 read=["CCC 552–553, 880–896","First Vatican Council, Pastor Aeternus (1870)","Oscar Cullmann (1952; Eng. 1953) Peter: Disciple, Apostle, Martyr. London: SCM","Stephen K. Ray (1999) Upon This Rock. San Francisco: Ignatius Press","D. A. Carson (1984) ‘Matthew’, in Expositor’s Bible Commentary vol. 8. Grand Rapids: Zondervan, on 16:18"])

D('matthew',23,9,
 ctx="Matthew 23 is Jesus’ great denunciation of the scribes and Pharisees. ‘They love the first places at feasts, and the first chairs in the synagogues, and salutations in the market place, and to be called by men, Rabbi. But be not you called Rabbi. For one is your master; and all you are brethren. And call none your father upon earth; for one is your father, who is in heaven. Neither be ye called masters; for one is your master, Christ. He that is the greatest among you shall be your servant’ (23:6–11). The chapter continues with the seven woes.",
 who="Catholics call priests ‘Father’ and the Pope ‘Holy Father’, directly disobeying Jesus’ command.",
 lang="Three titles are forbidden: ‘Rabbi’ (teacher), ‘father’ (patēr) and ‘master’ (kathēgētēs, guide, instructor; the modern Greek word for ‘professor’). If the verse is an absolute ban on the word, it bans ‘teacher’ and ‘instructor’ as much as ‘father’.",
 steps=[
  ["The point is pride, not vocabulary","The context is the scribes’ love of honours and titles (23:5–7). Jesus warns his disciples not to seek status or to put any human in God’s place as the ultimate source of life and truth. The conclusion: ‘he that is the greatest among you shall be your servant’ (23:11)."],
  ["Taken literally, it forbids ‘teacher’ and ‘doctor’ too","‘Be not you called Rabbi’ (teacher) and ‘neither be ye called masters’ (instructors). Protestants call their pastors ‘teacher’, their scholars ‘Doctor’, and speak of ‘the Founding Fathers’ and ‘church fathers’. Nobody applies the verse literally, because nobody thinks Jesus meant a ban on words."],
  ["The New Testament uses ‘father’ freely","Stephen addresses the Sanhedrin: ‘Ye men, brethren, and fathers, hear’ (Acts 7:2). Paul calls Abraham ‘the father of us all’ (Romans 4:16). Jesus himself, in a parable, has the rich man say ‘Father Abraham’ (Luke 16:24). John writes: ‘I write unto you, fathers’ (1 John 2:13). Jesus commands ‘Honour thy father and thy mother’ (Matthew 15:4)."],
  ["Paul calls himself a spiritual father","‘For if you have ten thousand instructors in Christ, yet not many fathers. For in Christ Jesus, by the gospel, I have begotten you’ (1 Corinthians 4:15). He calls Timothy ‘my dearly beloved son’ (2 Timothy 1:2) and Onesimus his son ‘whom I have begotten in my bands’ (Philemon 10). Spiritual fatherhood in Christ is apostolic."],
  ["All fatherhood comes from God’s","‘I bow my knees to the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, of whom all paternity (patria) in heaven and earth is named’ (Ephesians 3:14–15). Calling a priest ‘Father’ acknowledges a share in God’s fatherhood, as a natural father does. Jesus forbids treating any man as the source of what only God gives."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Jerome, Commentary on Matthew, on 23:9","One is our Father by nature; others are called fathers by grace or by age; the Lord forbids the pride of the title, not the honour of the office (paraphrased)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, Catena Aurea on Matthew 23","Collects Chrysostom and others: the command is against ambition and the usurpation of what belongs to God, not against the name (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Paul calling himself a father is different from a title.”","It is the same relationship. If someone can be called ‘my spiritual father’, he can be addressed that way. The early monks were called ‘Abba’ (father), and the Church Fathers have been called fathers by Protestants and Catholics alike for centuries."]],
 talk="“If Jesus meant we can never call anyone ‘father’, he also meant we can never call anyone ‘teacher’ or ‘instructor’, because they’re in the same sentence. Jesus was warning against the Pharisees’ love of titles and status. Paul says, ‘I became your father in Christ Jesus through the gospel’ (1 Corinthians 4:15). That’s why we call priests ‘Father’: they’re spiritual fathers, sharing in God’s fatherhood, not replacing it.”",
 avoid="Do not dismiss the verse; it is a real warning against clericalism and vanity, which Catholics should heed.",
 read=["CCC 2214–2215, 1547–1551","1 Corinthians 4:14–16","Thomas Aquinas, Catena Aurea on Matthew 23"])

D('1-timothy',4,3,
 ctx="1 Timothy 4:1–5: ‘Now the Spirit manifestly saith, that in the last times some shall depart from the faith, giving heed to spirits of error, and doctrines of devils, speaking lies in hypocrisy, and having their conscience seared, forbidding to marry, to abstain from meats, which God hath created to be received with thanksgiving by the faithful, and by them that have known the truth. For every creature of God is good, and nothing to be rejected that is received with thanksgiving: for it is sanctified by the word of God and prayer.’",
 who="The Catholic Church forbids priests to marry and requires abstinence from meat on Fridays in Lent (and penance on all Fridays). Therefore, it is argued, she teaches ‘doctrines of devils’ as Paul foretold.",
 lang="“Forbidding” (kōlyontōn) to marry: preventing marriage as such. The reason given is decisive: these things are forbidden as though God’s creation were not good (‘every creature of God is good’, 4:4).",
 steps=[
  ["Paul is condemning those who said marriage and food are evil","The false teachers forbade marriage and foods because they regarded the material creation as bad, hence Paul’s answer: ‘every creature of God is good’. This was the error of early Gnostic and Encratite sects (e.g. Marcion, Saturninus, Tatian’s followers), who taught that marriage was corruption and meat defiling. The Church fought these groups."],
  ["The Catholic Church teaches marriage is a sacrament","Far from forbidding marriage, the Church holds it to be one of the seven sacraments (CCC 1601–1666; Trent, Session 24). The Council of Gangra (c. 340) condemned anyone who despised marriage or abstained from meat out of contempt for creation. That is the error 1 Timothy describes, and the Church condemned it."],
  ["Celibacy is a free vocation, not a ban on marriage","No one is forced to become a priest. A man who chooses the priesthood in the Latin Church freely accepts celibacy as part of that vocation, as Paul recommends for those who can (1 Corinthians 7:7–8, 32–35) and as Christ described (‘eunuchs for the kingdom of heaven’, Matthew 19:12). The Eastern Catholic Churches ordain married men; celibacy for Latin priests is a discipline, not a doctrine that marriage is wrong (CCC 1579–1580)."],
  ["Fasting is a biblical practice, not contempt for food","Jesus said his disciples would fast (Matthew 9:15). Daniel abstained from meat and wine (Daniel 10:3). Paul says it is good not to eat meat if it causes a brother to stumble (Romans 14:21). Catholics abstain on certain days as penance precisely because meat is a good gift; giving up a good thing is a sacrifice, giving up an evil thing would not be."],
  ["Paul himself was celibate","‘I would that all men were even as myself’ (1 Corinthians 7:7). And he advises enrolled widows to keep their pledge not to remarry (1 Timothy 5:11–12). Paul can hardly be condemning voluntary celibacy."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Irenaeus, Against Heresies I.28.1 (c. 180)","Describes the Encratites, who following Saturninus and Marcion preached against marriage and abstained from animal food (paraphrased)."],
  ["Council of Gangra, canons 1–2 (c. 340)","Anathema to anyone who condemns marriage, or who condemns eating meat (with thanksgiving) as if it were sinful."],
  ["Augustine, On the Good of Marriage (401)","Marriage is good; virginity freely chosen for God is better; neither is evil (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Mandatory celibacy for priests is forbidding marriage.”","For those who freely choose a vocation that includes it, like a soldier accepting a posting where he cannot bring his family. No one is forbidden to marry; anyone may marry instead of being ordained. And the Church has married priests in Eastern rites and among some convert clergy."],
  ["“Many priests failed in celibacy, causing scandal.”","Sins against a vow show human weakness, not that the vow is evil, just as adultery does not prove marriage evil. The Church must and does address abuses."]],
 talk="“Paul is condemning people who said marriage and certain foods were evil, which is why he says ‘everything God created is good’. The Catholic Church teaches the exact opposite: marriage is a sacrament. Priests choose celibacy freely as part of a vocation, like Paul did, and Eastern Catholic priests can be married. And we abstain from meat on some days because it’s good; giving up something good is a sacrifice.”",
 avoid="Do not claim celibacy is of divine law for all priests; it is a discipline of the Latin Church (CCC 1580). Do not minimize real abuse or failures by clergy.",
 read=["CCC 1579–1580, 1601–1620, 2043","Council of Gangra (c. 340), canons","1 Corinthians 7","John Paul II (1979–84) Theology of the Body, catecheses on celibacy for the kingdom"])

# ---------------- New: Scripture and Tradition ----------------
add('acts',17,11,'Acts 17:11','The Bereans "searched the scriptures"',
 'The Bereans tested even an apostle by Scripture, and Luke praises them as "more noble". So every Christian should test every teaching, including the Church\'s, by Scripture alone, using private judgment.',
 ['The Bereans were Jews checking whether the Old Testament supported Paul\'s claim that Jesus was the Messiah. They had no New Testament.',
  'Having checked, they accepted Paul\'s oral preaching, which taught far more than the Old Testament said explicitly (the resurrection of Jesus, baptism, the Eucharist).',
  'Luke says they were "more noble" because they "received the word with all eagerness", in contrast to the Thessalonians, who rioted (17:5).',
  'Catholics also search the Scriptures and find there the faith the apostles preached. Searching Scripture is good; it is not the same as Scripture alone.'],
 'The Bereans are a model for reading Scripture eagerly and receiving the apostolic message, not for rejecting the apostles\' living authority.',
 'CCC 101–133; Dei Verbum 21–25',
 disp='d-2tim3',tags=('scripture',))
D('acts',17,11,
 ctx="Acts 17:1–15. In Thessalonica Paul ‘reasoned with them out of the scriptures, declaring and insinuating that the Christ was to suffer, and to rise again from the dead; and that this is Jesus Christ’ (17:2–3). Some believed, but jealous Jews raised a mob (17:5). Paul and Silas went to Berea. ‘Now these were more noble than those in Thessalonica, who received the word with all eagerness, daily searching the scriptures, whether these things were so. And many indeed of them believed’ (17:11–12). Then Jews from Thessalonica came to stir up trouble there too (17:13).",
 who="A favourite verse for sola scriptura and the right of private judgment: the Bereans, ordinary believers, did not accept an apostle’s word on authority but checked it against the Bible, and Scripture commends them for it. So Christians today should check the Pope and the Church against Scripture.",
 lang="“More noble” (eugenesteroi): of better disposition, more open-minded. The reason given is the participle clause: ‘who received (edexanto) the word with all eagerness (prothymias)’. ‘Searching’ (anakrinontes) means examining closely.",
 steps=[
  ["They were searching the Old Testament","The only Scriptures available were the Jewish Scriptures. Paul was arguing that they foretold a suffering and risen Messiah (17:3). Checking that claim against the prophets was exactly what Paul wanted. Catholics do the same: the Church has always argued from the Old Testament to Christ (Luke 24:27)."],
  ["They then accepted oral apostolic teaching","Having found the prophecies confirmed, ‘many of them believed’: they accepted Paul’s preaching, which went beyond what the Old Testament stated explicitly (the identity of the Messiah, his resurrection, the Church, baptism). The Bereans did not adopt ‘Old Testament alone’; they accepted a living apostle’s message as God’s word (cf. 1 Thessalonians 2:13: ‘you received it not as the word of men, but, as it is indeed, the word of God’)."],
  ["The praise is for their eagerness, not their method","Luke contrasts them with the Thessalonian Jews who formed a mob. ‘More noble’ because they listened with eagerness and examined fairly instead of rejecting Paul outright. The Thessalonians who did believe also examined the Scriptures (17:2–4)."],
  ["The verse does not describe settling doctrinal disputes among Christians","When Christians disagreed about doctrine (whether Gentiles must be circumcised), they did not each search Scripture and go their own way; they went ‘to the apostles and priests’ in Jerusalem, who decided in council (Acts 15:2, 6, 28). That is the model for disputes within the Church."],
  ["If the Bereans are the model of sola scriptura, the model fails","Applying their method strictly would mean accepting only what the Old Testament teaches, and rejecting any apostolic teaching not found there. No Protestant does that."]],
 fathers=[
  ["John Chrysostom, Homilies on Acts 37 (c. 400)","Praises the Bereans for receiving the word with readiness and examining it, not with hostility (paraphrased)."],
  ["Dei Verbum 25 (1965)","‘Ignorance of the Scriptures is ignorance of Christ’ (quoting Jerome): all the faithful are urged to read Scripture often."]],
 obj=[
  ["“The principle is: test everything by Scripture, which Catholics refuse.”","Catholics test teaching by Scripture and Tradition, read in the Church. Nothing the Church teaches contradicts Scripture. But ‘test by Scripture’ does not settle whose interpretation is right when sincere Bible-readers disagree; that requires an authority Scripture itself points to (Matthew 18:17; Acts 15; 1 Timothy 3:15)."]],
 talk="“The Bereans were Jews checking whether the Old Testament really foretold a suffering, rising Messiah, like Paul said. When they found it did, they believed Paul’s preaching, which taught a lot more than the Old Testament spelled out. Catholics search the Scriptures too, and they point us to Christ and to the Church he founded. When the early Christians had a disagreement about doctrine, in Acts 15, they didn’t each go to their Bibles separately; they went to the apostles in council.”",
 avoid="Do not discourage personal Bible study; the Church urges it (Dei Verbum 25).",
 read=["Dei Verbum 21–25","CCC 101–141","Acts 15"])

add('1-corinthians',4,6,'1 Corinthians 4:6','"Not above that which is written"',
 'Paul\'s phrase "not to go beyond what is written" is taken as a general rule: no Christian doctrine may go beyond the Bible. Catholic teachings not stated in Scripture break the rule.',
 ['The phrase is notoriously obscure. Paul applies it to the Corinthians\' pride in rival teachers: "that no one be puffed up for one against the other".',
  '"What is written" most likely means the Old Testament texts he has just quoted against human boasting (1:19, 31; 3:19–20).',
  'When Paul wrote, most of the New Testament did not exist. If the rule meant "Scripture alone", it would forbid accepting the Gospels and Paul\'s own later letters.',
  'In the same letter Paul praises the Corinthians for keeping the traditions he delivered orally (11:2).'],
 'Do not be puffed up in human leaders beyond what God\'s word says. It is a warning against pride, not a theory of the canon.',
 'CCC 76–83; 1 Corinthians 11:2',
 tags=('scripture','tradition'))
D('1-corinthians',4,6,
 ctx="1 Corinthians 1–4 addresses factions: ‘I indeed am of Paul; and I am of Apollo; and I of Cephas; and I of Christ’ (1:12). Paul insists that he and Apollos are only servants (3:5) and that the Corinthians should not boast in men (3:21). Then: ‘But these things, brethren, I have in a figure transferred to myself and to Apollo, for your sakes: that in us you may learn, that one be not puffed up against the other for another, above that which is written’ (4:6). He goes on: ‘For who distinguisheth thee? Or what hast thou that thou hast not received?’ (4:7).",
 who="Some Protestant writers use ‘not beyond what is written’ as an apostolic statement of sola scriptura: Paul teaches the church not to go beyond the written Scriptures in doctrine.",
 lang="The Greek is compressed: to mē hyper ha gegraptai, literally ‘the not beyond what is written’. It may be a slogan Paul is quoting, or a reference to his scriptural citations. Scholars disagree; some even suspect a scribal note entered the text. The phrase is introduced by ‘that you may learn in us’, and the purpose clause that follows is about not being ‘puffed up’ (physiousthe).",
 steps=[
  ["The application is to pride and factions","Paul states the purpose: ‘that one be not puffed up against the other for another’. The issue is boasting about Paul or Apollos. ‘Beyond what is written’ means: do not exalt teachers beyond what Scripture allows, for Scripture says ‘He that glorieth, let him glory in the Lord’ (1:31, quoting Jeremiah 9:23–24)."],
  ["‘What is written’ points to Paul’s Old Testament quotations","In chapters 1–3 Paul has repeatedly cited Scripture against human wisdom and boasting: Isaiah 29:14 (1:19), Jeremiah 9:24 (1:31), Isaiah 64:4 (2:9), Job 5:13 and Psalm 93:11 (3:19–20). ‘What is written’ is the formula he uses for those (gegraptai, ‘it is written’)."],
  ["A general sola scriptura reading proves too much","If Paul meant ‘believe nothing beyond the Scriptures you have’, then the Corinthians should have rejected his own later letter (2 Corinthians), the Gospels and every other New Testament book written afterwards. That cannot be his meaning."],
  ["The same letter commends oral tradition","‘Now I praise you, brethren, that in all things you are mindful of me: and keep my ordinances (paradoseis) as I have delivered them to you’ (11:2). He hands on the Eucharist (11:23) and the creed of the resurrection (15:3) as traditions received and delivered, and promises to set other matters in order ‘when I come’ (11:34)."]],
 fathers=[
  ["John Chrysostom, Homilies on 1 Corinthians 12 (c. 392)","Paul uses himself and Apollos as examples so the Corinthians would not be puffed up about their teachers (paraphrased)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on 1 Corinthians, ch. 4, lect. 1","‘Beyond what is written’: beyond what Scripture teaches about humility, that no one should glory in a man (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Even if obscure, the principle is that Scripture limits doctrine.”","Catholics agree that no doctrine may contradict Scripture, and that Scripture is the supreme written norm. But this verse doesn’t teach that only explicitly written doctrines may be held; Paul in this very letter relies on unwritten traditions."]],
 talk="“Paul is talking about the Corinthians being puffed up about their favourite preachers, ‘I’m with Paul, I’m with Apollos’, and he tells them not to go beyond what Scripture says about not boasting in men. If he meant ‘never believe anything beyond the written Bible’, they’d have had to reject the rest of the New Testament, which wasn’t written yet. And in chapter 11 he praises them for holding the traditions he passed on in person.”",
 avoid="Do not overstate how certain any reading of this phrase is; scholars agree it is difficult.",
 read=["Anthony Thiselton (2000) The First Epistle to the Corinthians. NIGTC. Grand Rapids: Eerdmans, pp. 351–356","Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on 1 Corinthians, ch. 4"])

add('galatians',1,8,'Galatians 1:8','"Though we, or an angel… preach another gospel… let him be anathema"',
 'Catholic additions (Mary, purgatory, saints, sacraments, merit) are "another gospel", and Paul curses anyone who preaches one.',
 ['Catholics accept the verse fully: no one, not even a pope or an angel, can change the gospel the apostles preached.',
  'The gospel Paul defends is the one he "preached" and they "received" (1:9): oral, apostolic teaching handed on, not a set of books.',
  'Paul himself went to Jerusalem to lay his gospel before Peter, James and John, "lest perhaps I should run, or had run in vain" (2:2).',
  'The real question is which community has kept the gospel as first received. Catholics point to continuity in doctrine from the apostolic era.'],
 'The gospel cannot be changed by anyone. The Church is its guardian, not its owner.',
 'Dei Verbum 7–10; CCC 84–87',
 tags=('scripture','tradition'))
D('galatians',1,8,
 ctx="Paul opens Galatians without his usual thanksgiving: ‘I wonder that you are so soon removed from him that called you into the grace of Christ, unto another gospel. Which is not another, only there are some that trouble you, and would pervert the gospel of Christ. But though we, or an angel from heaven, preach a gospel to you besides that which we have preached to you, let him be anathema. As we said before, so now I say again: If any one preach to you a gospel, besides that which you have received, let him be anathema’ (1:6–9). The ‘other gospel’ was the requirement of circumcision for Gentiles.",
 who="Used against any Catholic teaching considered unbiblical: it is ‘another gospel’. Sometimes used against Marian apparitions, or against papal teaching: if even an angel cannot change the gospel, neither can a pope.",
 lang="“Besides” (par’ ho): beyond, contrary to. “Anathema”: devoted to destruction, accursed; the term later used in councils to condemn heresy. “Received” (parelabete, 1:9) is the technical verb for receiving tradition (1 Corinthians 11:23; 15:1, 3).",
 steps=[
  ["Catholics wholeheartedly agree","The Church teaches that public revelation closed with the apostles and that the Magisterium ‘is not above the word of God, but serves it, teaching only what has been handed on’ (Dei Verbum 10). Vatican I: the Holy Spirit was not promised to Peter’s successors ‘that they might make known new doctrine’, but that they might guard and expound the deposit of faith (Pastor Aeternus, ch. 4). A pope who taught a new gospel would fall under Paul’s anathema."],
  ["The gospel was oral and received","Paul refers to what ‘we have preached’ and what ‘you have received’. At this point there was no New Testament. Paul is defending apostolic tradition, which included the sacraments: in the same letter, baptism ‘into Christ’ (3:27)."],
  ["The ‘other gospel’ was a specific error","Circumcision as a requirement for Gentiles, and with it the whole Law as the way of righteousness (5:2–4). Paul does not call every disputed practice ‘another gospel’. He tolerated disagreement on secondary matters (Romans 14)."],
  ["Paul submitted his gospel to Peter and the apostles","‘I went up… and conferred with them the gospel, which I preach among the Gentiles… lest perhaps I should run, or had run in vain’ (2:2). Earlier he had gone to see (historēsai) Cephas for fifteen days (1:18). Even an apostle sought communion with Peter."],
  ["The test is continuity","Which teaching matches the gospel received from the beginning? Catholics point to the early Christian witness on baptismal regeneration, the real presence, the sacrifice of the Mass, the authority of bishops in succession and prayer for the dead, all attested in the first three centuries. A gospel without these is the newer one."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Vincent of Lérins, Commonitorium 9.14 (434)","Comments on this verse: to announce to Catholic Christians anything other than what they have received never was, never is and never will be lawful (paraphrased)."],
  ["Tertullian, Prescription against Heretics 21 (c. 200)","What the apostles preached must be proved only through those churches which the apostles founded (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“The Church has added doctrines over time.”","The Church has defined doctrines over time, usually in response to challenges (as Nicaea did in 325). Development is not addition: Newman’s criteria (preservation of type, continuity of principles, logical sequence) distinguish growth from corruption (Essay on the Development of Christian Doctrine, 1845)."]],
 talk="“Catholics agree with Galatians 1:8 completely: not even a pope or an angel can change the gospel. The Church teaches the Pope can’t invent new doctrine. But notice Paul is defending the gospel the Galatians received by preaching, before there was a New Testament. So the question is: who has kept that apostolic gospel? Catholics point to the early Church: baptism that regenerates, the Eucharist as Christ’s body, bishops in succession, prayer for the dead. That’s the original gospel.”",
 avoid="Do not use ‘anathema’ language against fellow Christians lightly; Paul’s curse targeted a gospel-denying error.",
 read=["Dei Verbum 7–10","First Vatican Council, Pastor Aeternus ch. 4","John Henry Newman (1845) An Essay on the Development of Christian Doctrine","Vincent of Lérins, Commonitorium"])

add('colossians',2,8,'Colossians 2:8','"The tradition of men… and not according to Christ"',
 'Paul warns against "philosophy" and "the tradition of men". Catholic tradition and scholastic philosophy (Aristotle, Thomas Aquinas) are exactly what Paul condemned.',
 ['Paul condemns tradition and philosophy that are "according to the elements of the world, and not according to Christ". The qualifier matters.',
  'The context shows the Colossian error: food rules, festivals, worship of angels and harsh treatment of the body (2:16–23).',
  'Paul praises apostolic tradition elsewhere (2 Thessalonians 2:14; 1 Corinthians 11:2) and himself uses Greek thought when it serves the gospel (Acts 17:28).',
  'Faith and reason are allies: philosophy that serves Christ is not "vain deceit" (Fides et Ratio, 1998).'],
 'Tradition and philosophy that are against Christ are vain; tradition that hands on Christ, and reasoning that serves him, are gifts.',
 'Fides et Ratio 36–48; CCC 31–38, 75–83',
 tags=('scripture','tradition'))
D('colossians',2,8,
 ctx="Colossians 2 warns against a teaching that threatened the church at Colossae. ‘Beware lest any man cheat you by philosophy, and vain deceit; according to the tradition of men, according to the elements of the world, and not according to Christ: for in him dwelleth all the fulness of the Godhead corporeally’ (2:8–9). The rest of the chapter describes the error: ‘Let no man therefore judge you in meat or in drink, or in respect of a festival day, or of the new moon, or of the sabbaths’ (2:16); ‘Let no man seduce you, willing in humility, and religion of angels’ (2:18); ‘Touch not: taste not: handle not’ (2:21); ‘not in any honour to the filling of the flesh’ (2:23).",
 who="Used against Catholic Tradition generally, and against the use of Greek philosophy in Catholic theology (Thomism, transubstantiation’s use of ‘substance’). Sometimes against Catholic fasting rules as well.",
 lang="“Philosophy” (philosophia) here is qualified as ‘vain deceit’ (kenēs apatēs, ‘empty deception’). “Elements of the world” (stoicheia tou kosmou): either elementary religious principles or elemental spirits/powers, connected with the ‘religion of angels’ (2:18). The error appears to be a mix of Jewish calendar and food rules with mystical angel-veneration and ascetic rules.",
 steps=[
  ["Paul condemns a specific false system","The ‘philosophy’ is ‘according to the elements of the world, and not according to Christ’. It made Christ less than the fullness of God and put angels and rules in his place. Catholics condemn it too."],
  ["Not every tradition is ‘of men’","Paul distinguishes: tradition ‘of men’ that is ‘not according to Christ’ is vain; the tradition he and the apostles delivered must be held (2 Thessalonians 2:14; 3:6; 1 Corinthians 11:2). In Colossians itself he urges: ‘As therefore you have received (parelabete) Jesus Christ the Lord, walk ye in him’ (2:6), the language of receiving tradition."],
  ["Paul uses reason and even pagan poets","At Athens Paul quotes Aratus: ‘For we are also his offspring’ (Acts 17:28), and argues from creation in Romans 1:20. Christian thinkers from Justin Martyr onward used philosophy to defend and explain the faith. The First Vatican Council and John Paul II teach that faith and reason cannot contradict each other (Dei Filius 4; Fides et Ratio 1998)."],
  ["The ascetic rules Paul rejects were not Christian fasting","The Colossian rules (‘touch not, taste not’) sprang from contempt for the body and fear of spiritual powers. Christian fasting is done freely, for love of God, and affirms creation’s goodness (see note on 1 Timothy 4:3). Jesus said his disciples would fast (Matthew 9:15)."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Justin Martyr, Second Apology 13 (c. 155)","Whatever things were rightly said by any philosophers belong to us Christians, because of the seeds of the Word in all people (paraphrased)."],
  ["Clement of Alexandria, Stromata I.5 (c. 200)","Philosophy prepared the Greeks for Christ as the Law prepared the Jews (paraphrased)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on Colossians, ch. 2, lect. 2","Philosophy is condemned when it is followed against faith; using philosophical reasons in service of faith is not condemned (paraphrased)."],
  ["John Paul II, Fides et Ratio 1, 36–41 (1998)","Faith and reason are like two wings on which the human spirit rises to the truth."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Transubstantiation imports Aristotle into the gospel.”","The doctrine that the bread becomes Christ’s body is in Scripture and the Fathers long before scholastic terminology (see notes on John 6 and 1 Corinthians 11). ‘Substance’ simply names ‘what a thing is’, which ordinary language already distinguishes from appearances. The Church uses the word without canonizing a full Aristotelian system (Paul VI, Mysterium Fidei 24)."]],
 talk="“Paul is warning against a specific false teaching that put angels and food rules in place of Christ: ‘not according to Christ’ is the key phrase. In the same chapter he says ‘as you received Christ, walk in him’, using the word for receiving tradition. And Paul himself quoted pagan poets at Athens. Thinking carefully about the faith isn’t vain philosophy; it’s loving God with your mind.”",
 avoid="Do not imply that faith depends on Aristotle; the Church’s doctrines are revealed, and philosophy only helps explain them.",
 read=["John Paul II (1998) Fides et Ratio","First Vatican Council, Dei Filius ch. 4 (1870)","Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on Colossians, ch. 2"])

# ---------------- New: the Church ----------------
add('1-corinthians',3,11,'1 Corinthians 3:11','"Other foundation no man can lay… which is Christ Jesus"',
 'Christ is the only foundation of the Church, so Peter cannot be the rock on which it is built, and the papacy usurps Christ\'s place.',
 ['Scripture uses building images in more than one way. The same Paul writes that the Church is "built upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Jesus Christ himself being the chief corner stone" (Ephesians 2:20).',
  'Revelation 21:14: "the wall of the city had twelve foundations, and in them, the twelve names of the twelve apostles."',
  'Christ is the foundation by his own power; the apostles, and Peter as their head, are foundations by sharing in his work. The two images do not compete.',
  'In context, Paul is telling Corinthian teachers to build on Christ, not to boast in human leaders (3:4–10).'],
 'Christ is the one foundation; Peter is the rock by Christ\'s gift, so that the Church built on Christ stands firm.',
 'CCC 552–553, 857, 881; Ephesians 2:20; Revelation 21:14',
 disp='d-matt16',tags=('church',))
D('1-corinthians',3,11,
 ctx="1 Corinthians 3:5–15. Paul describes himself and Apollos as fellow workers: ‘I have planted, Apollo watered, but God gave the increase’ (3:6). ‘According to the grace of God that is given to me, as a wise architect, I have laid the foundation; and another buildeth thereon. But let every man take heed how he buildeth thereupon. For other foundation no man can lay, but that which is laid; which is Christ Jesus’ (3:10–11). Then: each builder’s work, whether gold, silver, precious stones, wood, hay or stubble, will be tested by fire (3:12–15).",
 who="This verse is used to answer Matthew 16:18: Christ alone is the rock and foundation; to call Peter the foundation of the Church contradicts Paul.",
 lang="“Foundation” (themelios). Ephesians 2:20 uses the same word: ‘built upon the foundation (themeliō) of the apostles and prophets’. Revelation 21:14: ‘twelve foundations (themelious)’.",
 steps=[
  ["Paul is speaking of preaching","In context, the ‘foundation’ Paul laid at Corinth is the preaching of Christ crucified (2:2), and other teachers must build on it with sound doctrine (3:10–15). He is not discussing church government."],
  ["Scripture calls the apostles foundations too","Ephesians 2:20 and Revelation 21:14 explicitly call the apostles the Church’s foundation. Since Scripture cannot contradict itself, ‘Christ is the only foundation’ and ‘the apostles are foundations’ must be true in different senses."],
  ["Christ is the primary foundation; the apostles are secondary, by participation","Thomas explains: Christ is the foundation by his own authority; the apostles by authority given them by Christ (Commentary on Ephesians, ch. 2, lect. 6). Peter is the rock in the same way: Christ is ‘the rock’ (1 Corinthians 10:4), and he names Simon ‘Rock’, giving him a share in his own stability (Luke 22:32)."],
  ["The same pattern applies to every title of Christ","Christ is the light of the world (John 8:12), yet says ‘you are the light of the world’ (Matthew 5:14). He is the good shepherd (John 10:11), yet tells Peter ‘feed my sheep’ (John 21:17). He is the one high priest, yet makes believers ‘a kingly priesthood’ (1 Peter 2:9). Sharing Christ’s titles by participation never rivals him."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Jerome, Against Jovinian I.26 (393)","The Church is founded on Peter, though elsewhere the same is said of all the apostles; yet one is chosen among the twelve so that, a head being appointed, the occasion of schism may be removed (paraphrased)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on 1 Corinthians, ch. 3, lect. 2","Christ is the foundation principally; the apostles and their teaching secondarily (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Ephesians 2:20 means the apostles’ teaching, not their persons.”","Revelation 21:14 puts the twelve names on the foundations. And teaching and teachers cannot be separated: the apostles taught with authority received from Christ (Luke 10:16: ‘He that heareth you, heareth me’)."]],
 talk="“Christ is absolutely the foundation of the Church. But Paul also says the Church is built on the foundation of the apostles and prophets (Ephesians 2:20), and Revelation puts the twelve apostles’ names on the foundations of the heavenly city. Jesus is the light of the world, and he tells us ‘you are the light of the world’. He shares his titles. Peter being the rock doesn’t compete with Christ; it’s Christ’s gift.”",
 avoid="Do not argue that Peter is the foundation ‘instead of’ Christ in any sense; the Catholic claim is participation.",
 read=["CCC 857–862, 880–882","Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on Ephesians, ch. 2, lect. 6","Ephesians 2:19–22"])

add('acts',10,26,'Acts 10:26','Peter: "Arise: I myself also am a man"',
 'When Cornelius knelt before Peter, Peter refused. So bowing to the Pope or kneeling before saints\' images is wrong, and Peter would have rejected the honours given to popes.',
 ['Cornelius, a Gentile, "falling at his feet adored" (prosekynēsen); Peter corrects what looked like worship of a man as a heavenly messenger.',
  'Catholics do not worship the Pope or the saints. Worship (latria) is for God alone (CCC 2096).',
  'Scripture also records honour rightly shown to God\'s servants: the sons of the prophets "worshipping him [Elisha] falling to the ground" (2 Kings 2:15) and Abigail bowing before David (1 Samuel 25:23).',
  'Peter\'s humility is the model for every pope; one of the papal titles is "servant of the servants of God".'],
 'Honour for God\'s servants is good; worship belongs to God alone. Peter\'s humility is the pattern for his successors.',
 'CCC 2096–2097, 2132; ST II-II q.84, q.103',
 tags=('church','saints'))
D('acts',10,26,
 ctx="Acts 10 tells of Cornelius, a Roman centurion, devout and God-fearing, who sees an angel telling him to send for Peter (10:3–6). Peter, meanwhile, has the vision of the sheet with unclean animals (10:9–16). When Peter arrives: ‘And it came to pass, that when Peter was come in, Cornelius came to meet him, and falling at his feet adored. But Peter lifted him up, saying: Arise, I myself also am a man’ (10:25–26). Peter then preaches, the Holy Spirit falls on the Gentiles, and Peter commands them to be baptized (10:44–48).",
 who="Used against kneeling before the Pope, kissing his ring and honours given to clergy, and against venerating saints and their images: if Peter, the first pope, refused such honour, Catholics are wrong to give it to his successors or to saints.",
 lang="“Adored” (prosekynēsen, from proskyneō): to prostrate oneself. The word can mean worship of God or the reverence given to a king or superior (Matthew 18:26; Revelation 3:9, where Christ says he will make enemies ‘come and adore (proskynēsousin) before thy feet’ of the faithful church).",
 steps=[
  ["Cornelius probably took Peter for more than a man","Cornelius had just seen an angel who sent him to Peter. A pagan-born Roman meeting the messenger announced by an angel could easily think him a divine or heavenly being. Peter’s answer, ‘I myself also am a man’, corrects exactly that. The same reaction is at Lystra, where the crowd want to sacrifice to Paul and Barnabas as gods (Acts 14:11–15)."],
  ["Prostration as honour is not always wrong","Revelation 3:9: Christ promises the faithful church in Philadelphia that its enemies will come and prostrate themselves (proskynēsousin) before their feet. In the Old Testament, the sons of the prophets bow to the ground before Elisha (2 Kings 2:15; 4 Kings in the Douay), Abigail before David (1 Samuel 25:23), Joseph’s brothers before Joseph (Genesis 42:6). What matters is the meaning of the act: worship of God or honour of a person."],
  ["The Church distinguishes worship from honour","Latria (adoration) is given to God alone; dulia (honour) to the saints; hyperdulia to Mary (ST II-II q.103 a.3–4; CCC 971, 2132). Respect shown to the Pope is civil and religious honour for the office of Christ’s vicar, not worship."],
  ["Peter’s humility binds his successors","The papal title servus servorum Dei, ‘servant of the servants of God’, used since Gregory the Great (590–604), expresses Christ’s command: ‘he that is the greater among you, let him become as the younger’ (Luke 22:26). In recent decades popes have simplified court ceremonial for this very reason."]],
 fathers=[
  ["John Chrysostom, Homilies on Acts 23 (c. 400)","Peter teaches humility: he does not accept honour beyond what is fitting, even when offered (paraphrased)."],
  ["Gregory the Great, Letters (c. 595)","Adopts the title ‘servant of the servants of God’ in protest against the title ‘universal bishop’ claimed by the patriarch of Constantinople (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Kissing the Pope’s ring is still too much.”","It is a customary gesture of respect for an office, like bowing to a monarch or kissing a bishop’s ring, and is optional; recent popes have often declined it. It is not part of doctrine."]],
 talk="“Cornelius had just seen an angel who told him to send for Peter, so when Peter came in he fell down as if before a heavenly being. Peter corrected him: ‘I’m only a man.’ Catholics agree: the Pope is a man and we don’t worship him. But Scripture shows people bowing to honour God’s servants, like the prophets bowing to Elisha. Honour and worship are different, and Peter’s humility is the model every pope is supposed to follow.”",
 avoid="Do not defend excessive court ceremonial; the Church herself has reformed it. Stick to the distinction between honour and worship.",
 read=["CCC 2096–2097, 2132, 971","ST II-II q.84 a.1; q.103 a.3–4","Revelation 3:9"])

add('revelation',17,9,'Revelation 17:9','Babylon "sitteth on seven mountains"',
 'The great harlot Babylon sits on seven hills; Rome is the city of seven hills; therefore the harlot is the Roman Catholic Church and the Pope is the Antichrist.',
 ['In John\'s day, "the great city which hath kingdom over the kings of the earth" (17:18) was pagan imperial Rome, which was killing Christians.',
  'The woman is "drunk with the blood of the saints, and with the blood of the martyrs of Jesus" (17:6): the persecuting empire of Nero and Domitian, not the Church whose saints those martyrs were.',
  'Peter, writing from Rome, sends greetings from "the church that is in Babylon" (1 Peter 5:13). For the early Christians, "Babylon" was a code-name for imperial Rome.',
  'The Church of Rome is the Church of the martyrs Peter and Paul, built on their tombs. The harlot is the power that killed them.'],
 'Babylon is the persecuting world-empire of John\'s day, and every power that makes war on the saints; it is not the Church of the martyrs.',
 'CCC 675–677; 1 Peter 5:13; Revelation 17–18',
 tags=('church',))
D('revelation',17,9,
 ctx="Revelation 17 describes ‘the great harlot that sitteth upon many waters’ (17:1), seated on a scarlet beast with seven heads and ten horns, dressed in purple and scarlet, holding a golden cup, ‘and on her forehead a name was written: A mystery; Babylon the great, the mother of the fornications, and the abominations of the earth. And I saw the woman drunk with the blood of the saints, and with the blood of the martyrs of Jesus’ (17:5–6). The angel explains: ‘The seven heads are seven mountains, upon which the woman sitteth, and they are seven kings: five are fallen, one is, and the other is not yet come’ (17:9–10). ‘And the woman which thou sawest, is the great city, which hath kingdom over the kings of the earth’ (17:18). Chapter 18 describes the fall of Babylon, a commercial empire whose merchants grew rich from her (18:11–19).",
 who="From the Reformation onwards (Luther, Calvin, the Westminster Confession of 1646, which called the Pope ‘that Antichrist, that man of sin’, XXV.6, a clause later removed by many Presbyterian bodies), and in modern fundamentalist literature, the harlot is identified with the Catholic Church: Rome is built on seven hills; the colours purple and scarlet are those of bishops and cardinals; the golden cup is the chalice; she has shed the blood of Protestant martyrs.",
 lang="“Mountains” (orē) and “kings” (basileis): the seven heads have a double meaning. Roman writers routinely called Rome ‘the city of seven hills’ (Virgil, Horace, Cicero). “Five are fallen, one is”: a reference to the line of Roman emperors at the time of writing. The ‘number of the beast’ 666 (13:18) is most commonly explained by scholars as the numerical value of ‘Nero Caesar’ in Hebrew letters (nrwn qsr); some manuscripts read 616, which matches the Latin spelling ‘Nero’.",
 steps=[
  ["John is addressing seven churches under persecution in the 1st century","Revelation is written to Christians in Asia Minor (1:4, 11), suffering under Rome. Antipas has been killed (2:13); John is exiled on Patmos ‘for the word of God’ (1:9). The ‘beast’ demands worship (13:4, 15), as the imperial cult did. Revelation’s immediate meaning concerns that empire."],
  ["Babylon was early Christian code for pagan Rome","1 Peter 5:13: ‘The church that is in Babylon… saluteth you’, written from Rome. Jewish writings after AD 70 (4 Ezra, 2 Baruch, the Sibylline Oracles) call Rome ‘Babylon’, since like Babylon it destroyed Jerusalem and the Temple. The seven hills, ‘five kings fallen’ and ‘the great city which hath kingdom over the kings of the earth’ (17:18) all fit imperial Rome in John’s day."],
  ["The harlot kills the martyrs; the Church is the Church of the martyrs","The woman is ‘drunk with the blood of the saints and martyrs of Jesus’ (17:6). Peter and Paul were killed in Rome under Nero (1 Clement 5; Tertullian, Prescription 36; Eusebius II.25). The Roman Church is built on their tombs. To identify the persecutor with the persecuted Church turns the image upside down."],
  ["The Church in Revelation is a different woman","Revelation 12 shows a woman ‘clothed with the sun’, who gives birth to the Messiah and is persecuted by the dragon; and 19:7–8; 21:2, 9 show the Bride of the Lamb, the holy city Jerusalem. Babylon is the anti-Church: the world in its power, wealth and idolatry, which every age must resist, including Christians tempted by wealth and power."],
  ["Some Catholic commentators also read a warning to Christians","Catholic interpretation has also seen in Babylon every worldly power that seduces and persecutes the faithful, and even unfaithful Jerusalem (11:8, ‘where their Lord also was crucified’). The warning ‘Go out from her, my people’ (18:4) applies to Christians in every age, Catholics included. But nothing in the text identifies the Church of the martyrs with the harlot."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Tertullian, Against Marcion III.13 and Answer to the Jews 9 (c. 200–210)","Says Babylon in John is a figure of the city of Rome, mighty and proud and the persecutor of the saints (paraphrased)."],
  ["Augustine, City of God XVIII.2, 22 (c. 420)","Calls Rome ‘a second Babylon’, the earthly city that persecutes the city of God (paraphrased)."],
  ["Haydock’s Catholic Bible Commentary (1811–14), on Revelation 17","Follows the Fathers in reading Babylon as pagan Rome, persecutor of Christians (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“The colours purple and scarlet are cardinals’ colours.”","Purple and scarlet were the colours of wealth and royalty in the ancient world (Mark 15:17; Luke 16:19). Cardinals’ red is meant to signify readiness to shed blood for Christ, and dates from the 13th century. You cannot interpret a 1st-century text by medieval vestments."],
  ["“The Catholic Church persecuted Protestants.”","Catholics and Protestants both persecuted one another in the 16th and 17th centuries, and Catholics have acknowledged their share (John Paul II, Jubilee day of pardon, 12 March 2000). But Revelation 17 is not about the Reformation; it describes the empire killing ‘the martyrs of Jesus’ in John’s own day."],
  ["“Vatican City is on Vatican Hill, which is one of the seven hills.”","The Vatican Hill is not among Rome’s classical seven hills (Palatine, Capitoline, Aventine, Caelian, Esquiline, Quirinal, Viminal); it lies across the Tiber, outside the ancient city."]],
 talk="“John is writing to Christians being persecuted by the Roman Empire. The early Christians called Rome ‘Babylon’; Peter even signs off his letter from Rome as ‘the church in Babylon’. The woman is drunk with the blood of the martyrs, and Rome killed Peter and Paul. The Catholic Church in Rome is built over their graves. So the harlot is the empire that killed the martyrs, not the Church that honours them.”",
 avoid="Do not get drawn into date-setting or speculative end-times charts. Do not deny that Christians, including Catholics, have sometimes behaved like ‘Babylon’; the Church calls her members to conversion.",
 read=["CCC 675–677","Richard Bauckham (1993) The Theology of the Book of Revelation. Cambridge: CUP, ch. 2 (an Anglican scholar on Babylon as Rome)","Scott Hahn (1999) The Lamb’s Supper. New York: Doubleday","Haydock Commentary on Revelation 17"])
